## Summary

The public English text front end turns `$1.5` into "one dollar and five cents", even though the amount equals `$1.50`. Both `normalize` and `phonemize` instead make `$1.5` identical to `$1.05`. The same probe finds "two dollars and five cents" for `$2.5`, and the failure also occurs inside a sentence. This changes the numerical meaning of the script before speech synthesis.

## Steps to reproduce

Use a checkout of the baseline under Runtime or environment and Rust/Cargo. From the checkout root, run the following complete shell script. It compiles a small external binary with a path dependency on the actual public crate. The calls use American English, an empty lexicon and default pronunciation overrides. No voice model, GUI, media file or optional neural-voice build is needed.

```sh
#!/bin/sh
set -eu
FILMCRAFT_CHECKOUT=$PWD
MONEY_PROBE_DIR=$(mktemp -d)
mkdir "$MONEY_PROBE_DIR/src"
cat > "$MONEY_PROBE_DIR/Cargo.toml" <<TOML
[package]
name = "money-reading-probe"
version = "0.0.0"
edition = "2024"

[dependencies]
filmcraft-tts-text = { path = "$FILMCRAFT_CHECKOUT/crates/tts-text" }

[workspace]
TOML
cat > "$MONEY_PROBE_DIR/src/main.rs" <<'RUST'
use filmcraft_tts_text::{Lang, Lexicon, Overrides, Token, normalize, phonemize};

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
