1. **Paste always targets the timeline**, regardless of which panel has focus. With focus in Effect Controls or the Effects panel, `Cmd+V` still pastes at the playhead.
2. **On macOS, paste inserts the content twice.**
3. **There is no way to copy an effect from clip A onto clip B.** Effects cannot be copied, pasted or duplicated on their own — you have to go through Paste Attributes with a whole clip in the clipboard.

(1) and (3) are structural and reproducible from the code. (2) I could not reproduce statically — it needs a real macOS run — but there are two unguarded dispatch paths that both end in `paste()`, which is the most likely cause; details and a repro recipe below.

### 1. Paste ignores UI focus

`paste()` in `crates/engine/src/commands.rs:3642-3674` picks its destination from the playhead and the active sequence only:

```rust
fn paste(s: &mut Session, insert: bool) -> Result<Value> {
    let at = s.playhead();
    let clip = s.state.clipboard.clone();
    ...
    it.start += at;
```

Nothing in the function reads `focused`, the panel, or the selected effect. Copy is the same story: `copy_selection` (`commands.rs:3617`) reads only `s.state.selection`, i.e. the timeline selection. And `edit.paste` is enabled purely on clip count (`has_clipboard`, `commands.rs:120-123`), so it is never greyed out when focus is elsewhere.

There *is* one focus-aware branch in the UI layer, but it only special-cases media from the system clipboard (`crates/ui-egui/src/menus.rs:533-543`):

```rust
"edit.paste" | "edit.pasteInsert"
    if params.as_object().is_none_or(|m| m.is_empty()) && matches!(app.ui.focused, PanelKind::Timeline | PanelKind::Project) =>
{
    if let Some(r) = paste_clipboard_media(app, id == "edit.pasteInsert") {
        return r;
    }
}
```

